+## RCTD
+
+Next, we perform deconvolution with `r BiocStyle::Githubpkg("dmcable/spacexr")` (f.k.a. RCTD)[@Cable2022-RCTD]. [Note that RCTD can also be adapted to VisiumHD data with `doublet_mode="doublet"`, as demonstrated by [@Oliveira2024] and @sec-seq-workflow-visiumhd.]{.aside} By default, `run.RCTD()`'s `doublet_mode="doublet"`, specifies at most two subpopulations coexist in a data unit, such as a "spot"; here, we set `doublet_mode="full"` in order to allow for an arbitrary number of subpopulations to be fit instead.
+
+```{r decvis, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, results='hide'}
+xy <- data.frame(spatialCoords(vis))
+.vis <- SpatialRNA(xy, counts(vis))
+ids <- factor(sce$Annogrp)
+names(ids) <- colnames(sce)
+ref <- Reference(counts(sce), ids)
+res <- create.RCTD(.vis, ref, max_cores=10)
+res <- run.RCTD(res, doublet_mode="full")
+```
+
+Weights inferred by `RCTD` should be normalized such that proportions of cell types sum to 1 in each spot:
+
+```{r dec-res, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE}
+# normalize weights to obtain proportions
+ws <- normalize_weights(res@results$weights)
+# add proportion estimates as spot metadata
+ws <- data.frame(as.matrix(ws))
+colData(vis)[names(ws)] <- ws[colnames(vis), ]
+```
+
+Let's visualize deconvolution weights in space, i.e., coloring by the proportion of a given cell type estimated to fall within a given spot:
+
+```{r plt-dec-xy, message=FALSE, fig.width=10, fig.height=5}
+#| code-fold: true
+lapply(names(ws), \(.) 
+    plotSpots(vis, annotate=., point_size = 0.05)) |>
+    wrap_plots(nrow=3) & theme(
+    legend.key.width=unit(0.5, "lines"),
+    legend.key.height=unit(1, "lines")) &
+    scale_color_gradientn(colors=pals::jet())
+```
+
+The deconvolution result can be viewed as a heatmap:
 
-# plot number of cells per spot
-plotSpotQC(spe, plot_type = "histogram", x_metric = "cell_count") + 
-  xlab("number of cells") + 
-  ggtitle("Number of cells per spot")
+```{r heatmap, fig.width=4, fig.height=3}
+#| code-fold: true
+pheatmap(ws, show_rownames=FALSE, show_colnames=TRUE,
+    cellwidth=12, treeheight_row=5, treeheight_col=5)
 ```
 
+We see that more than half of the spots have stromal cell type proportion of more than 50%, according to RCTD. Few spots have intense and distinct signal for tumor, DCIS1 and DCIS2.
+
+For comparison with spot annotations provided by 10x Genomics, we include majority voted cell type from deconvolution as `RCTD`. Note that because stromal cells show broad signals across the entire tissue, to better investigate immune cell signals, we remove stromal from the majority vote calculation for an alternative RCTD label as `RCTD_no_stroma`.
+
+```{r dec-clu}
+# derive majority vote label
+ids <- names(ws)[apply(ws, 1, which.max)]
+names(ids) <- rownames(ws)
+vis$RCTD <- factor(ids[colnames(vis)])
+
+# derive majority vote nouding stromal cells
+ws_no_stroma <- ws[, colnames(ws) != "stromal"]
+ids_no_stroma <- names(ws_no_stroma)[apply(ws_no_stroma, 1, which.max)]
+names(ids_no_stroma) <- rownames(ws)
+vis$RCTD_no_stroma <- factor(ids_no_stroma[colnames(vis)])
+```
+
+We can visualize these three annotations spatially.
+
+```{r plt-clu-xy, message=FALSE, fig.width=10, fig.height=2.5}
